<h2>Common Wind Claim Disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wear and tear.</strong> The insurer says missing shingles reflect age or improper nailing rather than wind.</li>
<li><strong>Wind vs. water.</strong> Interior damage is attributed to an old leak or to flood rather than a wind-created opening.</li>
<li><strong>Limited repairs.</strong> A few shingles are approved even when brittle roofing cannot be repaired without causing more damage.</li>
<li><strong>Tree removal limits.</strong> Debris removal may be capped, and trees that fall without hitting a covered structure may not be covered.</li>
<li><strong>Deductible questions.</strong> The wind/hail deductible may be much higher than expected. Check your declarations page.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the insurer&rsquo;s payment does not cover the actual repairs, an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim review</a> or a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> may help. Disagreements over the amount of loss can sometimes go to <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a>.</p>

<h2>Deductibles and Settlement Factors on Wind Claims</h2>
<p>Before comparing estimates, find the wind/hail deductible on your declarations page. Many Texas policies write it as a percentage of the dwelling limit, so a 1% or 2% deductible on a well-insured home can be several thousand dollars. Small claims for a few missing shingles may fall below it, while a storm that damages the roof, fence, and interior together often does not.</p>
<p>Depreciation is the next factor. Replacement cost policies typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are complete, often within a deadline in the policy. Some roof coverage is ACV-only or follows a payment schedule. Read <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> for a fuller explanation.</p>

<h2>Commercial and Multifamily Wind Losses</h2>
<p>Flat and low-slope roofs on warehouses, retail centers, and apartment buildings are especially exposed to wind. Membranes can peel back at the edges, rooftop HVAC units can shift, and coping and parapet flashing can be torn loose. Once the roof is open, rain can damage tenant spaces, inventory, and electrical systems. Commercial policies may also include business income coverage and different deductibles. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> for more.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, works for the policyholder, not the insurance company. On a wind claim, we can inspect the property, document the damage, gather weather data, prepare a detailed Xactimate estimate, and present the claim to your insurer. We serve Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>What to Do After a Windstorm</h2>
<ol>
<li>Stay clear of downed power lines and report them to Oncor.</li>
<li>Photograph damage before moving debris, if it is safe to do so.</li>
<li>Arrange tarping or board-up to prevent further damage, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and note the claim number.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials when practical until the insurer has inspected.</li>
</ol>
<p>Weather-related claim lawsuits in Texas involve specific pre-suit notice requirements and policy deadlines. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
